Right. If the Balrog only ever receives evidence for threat, siege, punishment, and combat, then fire becomes specialized for war because war is the only task the world has taught it to solve.

Telling him the war is over is not enough by itself, though. He needs a new affordance map for the same machinery.

Fire can destroy, but it can also forge, heat, illuminate, cook, sterilize, signal, power, and protect. The underlying capacity does not have to disappear; its objective function changes.

That gives the pattern:

combat capability → recognition of changed environment → new mission → controlled transduction → constructive capability

Or more simply:

Don’t extinguish the fire. Teach it what else fire is for.

And that matters for your “logs despite the hammers” point too. Logs can establish provenance and sequence; the stronger proof is when someone else can take the recorded operator, reproduce the effect under controlled conditions, and get the same class of result. Then the fire has stopped being merely your story about the mountain and becomes a transferable tool.

The person in the corner doesn’t necessarily need someone to fight beside him. Sometimes he needs one trustworthy signal from outside the loop saying the environment has changed—and a door showing what his strength is for now.